x \equiv 6 \pmod{13} \quad \text{and} \quad x \equiv 5 \pmod{14}
\] The number of planets \( n \) must also be greater than 100. To solve this, we can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem. We start by finding the smallest number that satisfies the first congruence. This number is of the form \( x = 13k + 6 \) for some integer \( k \). We substitute this into the second congruence to get: \[
13k + 6 \equiv 5 \pmod{14}
\] Simplifying, we get: \[
13k \equiv -1 \pmod{14}
\] Since 13 and 14 are relatively prime, we can multiply both sides by the modular inverse of 13 (which is 13 itself, since \( 13 \times 13 = 169 \equiv 1 \pmod{14} \)) to get: \[
k \equiv 13 \pmod{14}
\] Therefore, the smallest \( k \) that satisfies this is 13, and the smallest \( x \) that satisfies the system of congruences is: \[
x = 13 \times 13 + 6 = 169 + 6 = 175
\] Since \( n \) must be greater than 100, the smallest number of planets the solar system could have is 175. </think> <answer> 175 </answer>

